            <ArticleTitle>Headache, thrombolytic therapy, and chronic subdural hemorrhage--a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>77-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Subdural hematomas are well known but infrequent complications of thrombolytic therapy. Although these are usually associated with head trauma, the authors describe a case of a patient who received a particularly aggressive thrombolytic regimen and presented six months later with complaints of nothing more than a headache resistant to medical therapy, without associated neurologic manifestations, which was finally diagnosed as a chronic subdural hematoma by computerized tomography. In the era of thrombolysis, physicians should maintain a heightened index of suspicion for subdural hematoma in patients complaining of headache.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
